/// The [execution environment](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html)
/// options shared by the sections of the unit types that execute commands.
///
/// Unit configuration files for services, sockets, mount points, and swap
/// devices share a subset of configuration options which define the execution
/// environment of spawned processes.
///
/// This module is not a unit by itself: it is the base class of the section
/// classes that accept these options, such as `ServiceSection` in
/// `Service.pkl`. It extends `Kill`, and through it `ResourceControl`, because
/// every unit type that accepts the execution environment options also accepts
/// the kill and resource control options.
///
/// Unless a property says otherwise, a `Listing` is rendered as one line per
/// element. systemd merges such lines into a single list, and each element may
/// itself hold several values in the syntax the option accepts, for instance
/// separated by spaces.
abstract module systemd.Exec

extends "./Kill.pkl"

import "./Unit.pkl"

/// Takes a colon separated list of absolute paths relative to which the
/// executable used by the `Exec*=` (e.g. `ExecStart=`, `ExecStop=`, etc.)
/// properties can be found. `ExecSearchPath=` overrides `$PATH` if `$PATH` is
/// not supplied by the user through `Environment=`, `EnvironmentFile=` or
/// `PassEnvironment=`. Assigning an empty string removes previous assignments
/// and setting `ExecSearchPath=` to a value multiple times will append to the
/// previous setting.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `ExecSearchPath=`
/// line.
///
/// See [ExecSearchPath=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#ExecSearchPath=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 250.
execSearchPath: Listing<String>?

/// Takes a directory path relative to the service's root directory specified
/// by `RootDirectory=`, or the special value `~`. Sets the working directory
/// for executed processes. If set to `~`, the home directory of the user
/// specified in `User=` is used. If not set, defaults to the root directory
/// when systemd is running as a system instance and the respective user's home
/// directory if run as user. If the setting is prefixed with the `-`
/// character, a missing working directory is not considered fatal. If
/// `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` is not set, then `WorkingDirectory=` is
/// relative to the root of the system running the service manager. Note that
/// setting this parameter might result in additional dependencies to be added
/// to the unit (see above).
///
/// See [WorkingDirectory=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#WorkingDirectory=) in systemd.exec(5).
workingDirectory: String?

/// Takes a directory path relative to the host's root directory (i.e. the root
/// of the system running the service manager). Sets the root directory for
/// executed processes, with the pivot_root(2) or chroot(2) system call. If
/// this is used, it must be ensured that the process binary and all its
/// auxiliary files are available in the new root. Note that setting this
/// parameter might result in additional dependencies to be added to the unit
/// (see above).
///
/// The `MountAPIVFS=` and `PrivateUsers=` settings are particularly useful in
/// conjunction with `RootDirectory=`. For details, see below.
///
/// If `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` are used together with `NotifyAccess=` the
/// notification socket is automatically mounted from the host into the root
/// environment, to ensure the notification interface can work correctly.
///
/// Note that services using `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` will not be able to
/// log via the syslog or journal protocols to the host logging infrastructure,
/// unless the relevant sockets are mounted from the host, specifically:
///
/// The host's os-release(5) file will be made available for the service
/// (read-only) as `/run/host/os-release`. It will be updated automatically on
/// soft reboot (see: systemd-soft-reboot.service(8)), in case the service is
/// configured to survive it.
///
/// **Example: Mounting logging sockets into root environment**
///
/// ```
/// BindReadOnlyPaths=/dev/log /run/systemd/journal/socket /run/systemd/journal/stdout
/// ```
///
/// In place of the directory path a `.v/` versioned directory may be
/// specified, see systemd.v(7) for details.
///
/// This option is only available for system services, or for services running
/// in per-user instances of the service manager in which case `PrivateUsers=`
/// is implicitly enabled (requires unprivileged user namespaces support to be
/// enabled in the kernel via the `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl).
///
/// See [RootDirectory=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootDirectory=) in systemd.exec(5).
rootDirectory: String?

/// Takes a path to a block device node or regular file as argument. This call
/// is similar to `RootDirectory=` however mounts a file system hierarchy from
/// a block device node or loopback file instead of a directory. The device
/// node or file system image file needs to contain a file system without a
/// partition table, or a file system within an MBR/MS-DOS or GPT partition
/// table with only a single Linux-compatible partition, or a set of file
/// systems within a GPT partition table that follows the [UAPI.2 Discoverable
/// Partitions
/// Specification](https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/discoverable_partitions_specification).
///
/// When `DevicePolicy=` is set to `closed` or `strict`, or set to `auto` and
/// `DeviceAllow=` is set, then this setting adds `/dev/loop-control` with `rw`
/// mode, `block-loop` and `block-blkext` with `rwm` mode to `DeviceAllow=`.
/// See systemd.resource-control(5) for the details about `DevicePolicy=` or
/// `DeviceAllow=`. Also, see `PrivateDevices=` below, as it may change the
/// setting of `DevicePolicy=`.
///
/// Units making use of `RootImage=` automatically gain an `After=` dependency
/// on `systemd-udevd.service`.
///
/// The host's os-release(5) file will be made available for the service
/// (read-only) as `/run/host/os-release`. It will be updated automatically on
/// soft reboot (see: systemd-soft-reboot.service(8)), in case the service is
/// configured to survive it.
///
/// In place of the image path a `.v/` versioned directory may be specified,
/// see systemd.v(7) for details.
///
/// When enabled for services running in per-user instances of the service
/// manager this option implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` (requires
/// unprivileged user namespaces support to be enabled in the kernel via the
/// `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl) and also relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8).
///
/// See [RootImage=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootImage=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 233.
rootImage: String?

/// Takes a comma-separated list of mount options that will be used on disk
/// images specified by `RootImage=`. Optionally a partition name can be
/// prefixed, followed by colon, in case the image has multiple partitions,
/// otherwise partition name `root` is implied. Options for multiple partitions
/// can be specified in a single element with space separators. For a list of
/// valid mount options, please refer to mount(8).
///
/// Valid partition names follow the [Discoverable Partitions
/// Specification](https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/discoverable_partitions_specification):
/// `root`, `usr`, `home`, `srv`, `esp`, `xbootldr`, `tmp`, `var`.
///
/// This option is only available for system services and is not supported for
/// services running in per-user instances of the service manager, where using
/// mount options is disabled by default, due to the security implications. A
/// [polkit](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/polkit/docs/latest/) policy
/// may be used to allow specific mount options there.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `RootImageOptions=`
/// line.
///
/// See [RootImageOptions=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootImageOptions=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 247.
rootImageOptions: Listing<String>?

/// Takes a boolean argument. If enabled, executed processes will run in an
/// ephemeral copy of the root directory or root image. The ephemeral copy is
/// placed in `/var/lib/systemd/ephemeral-trees/` while the service is active
/// and is cleaned up when the service is stopped or restarted. If
/// `RootDirectory=` is used and the root directory is a subvolume, the
/// ephemeral copy will be created by making a snapshot of the subvolume.
///
/// To make sure making ephemeral copies can be made efficiently, the root
/// directory or root image should be located on the same filesystem as
/// `/var/lib/systemd/ephemeral-trees/`. When using `RootEphemeral=` with root
/// directories, btrfs(5) should be used as the filesystem and the root
/// directory should ideally be a subvolume which **systemd** can snapshot to
/// make the ephemeral copy. For root images, a filesystem with support for
/// reflinks should be used to ensure an efficient ephemeral copy.
///
/// This option is only available for system services and is not supported for
/// services running in per-user instances of the service manager.
///
/// See [RootEphemeral=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootEphemeral=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 254.
rootEphemeral: Boolean?

/// Takes a data integrity (dm-verity) root hash specified in hexadecimal, or
/// the path to a file containing a root hash in ASCII hexadecimal format. This
/// option enables data integrity checks using dm-verity, if the used image
/// contains the appropriate integrity data (see above) or if `RootVerity=` is
/// used. The specified hash must match the root hash of integrity data, and is
/// usually at least 256 bits (and hence 64 formatted hexadecimal characters)
/// long (in case of SHA256 for example). If this option is not specified, but
/// the image file carries the `user.verity.roothash` extended file attribute
/// (see xattr(7)), then the root hash is read from it, also as formatted
/// hexadecimal characters. If the extended file attribute is not found (or is
/// not supported by the underlying file system), but a file with the
/// `.roothash` suffix is found next to the image file, bearing otherwise the
/// same name (except if the image has the `.raw` suffix, in which case the
/// root hash file must not have it in its name), the root hash is read from it
/// and automatically used, also as formatted hexadecimal characters.
///
/// If the disk image contains a separate `/usr/` partition it may also be
/// Verity protected, in which case the root hash may configured via an
/// extended attribute `user.verity.usrhash` or a `.usrhash` file adjacent to
/// the disk image. There's currently no option to configure the root hash for
/// the `/usr/` file system via the unit file directly.
///
/// When enabled for services running in per-user instances of the service
/// manager this option implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` (requires
/// unprivileged user namespaces support to be enabled in the kernel via the
/// `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl) and also relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8).
///
/// See [RootHash=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootHash=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 246.
rootHash: String?

/// Takes a PKCS7 signature of the `RootHash=` option as a path to a
/// DER-encoded signature file, or as an ASCII base64 string encoding of a
/// DER-encoded signature prefixed by `base64:`. The dm-verity volume will only
/// be opened if the signature of the root hash is valid and signed by a public
/// key present in the kernel keyring. If this option is not specified, but a
/// file with the `.roothash.p7s` suffix is found next to the image file,
/// bearing otherwise the same name (except if the image has the `.raw` suffix,
/// in which case the signature file must not have it in its name), the
/// signature is read from it and automatically used.
///
/// If the disk image contains a separate `/usr/` partition it may also be
/// Verity protected, in which case the signature for the root hash may
/// configured via a `.usrhash.p7s` file adjacent to the disk image. There's
/// currently no option to configure the root hash signature for the `/usr/`
/// via the unit file directly.
///
/// When enabled for services running in per-user instances of the service
/// manager this option implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` (requires
/// unprivileged user namespaces support to be enabled in the kernel via the
/// `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl) and also relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8).
///
/// See [RootHashSignature=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootHashSignature=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 246.
rootHashSignature: String?

/// Takes the path to a data integrity (dm-verity) file. This option enables
/// data integrity checks using dm-verity, if `RootImage=` is used and a
/// root-hash is passed and if the used image itself does not contain the
/// integrity data. The integrity data must be matched by the root hash. If
/// this option is not specified, but a file with the `.verity` suffix is found
/// next to the image file, bearing otherwise the same name (except if the
/// image has the `.raw` suffix, in which case the verity data file must not
/// have it in its name), the verity data is read from it and automatically
/// used.
///
/// This option is supported only for disk images that contain a single file
/// system, without an enveloping partition table. Images that contain a GPT
/// partition table should instead include both root file system and matching
/// Verity data in the same image, implementing the [Discoverable Partitions
/// Specification](https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/discoverable_partitions_specification).
///
/// When enabled for services running in per-user instances of the service
/// manager this option implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` (requires
/// unprivileged user namespaces support to be enabled in the kernel via the
/// `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl) and also relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8).
///
/// See [RootVerity=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootVerity=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 246.
rootVerity: String?

/// Takes an image policy string as per systemd.image-policy(7) to use when
/// mounting the disk images (DDI) specified in `RootImage=`, `MountImage=`,
/// `ExtensionImage=`, respectively. If not specified the following policy
/// string is the default for `RootImagePolicy=` and `MountImagePolicy`:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         home=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         srv=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         tmp=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         var=encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// The default policy for `ExtensionImagePolicy=` is:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// See [RootImagePolicy=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootImagePolicy=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 254.
rootImagePolicy: String?

/// Takes an image policy string as per systemd.image-policy(7) to use when
/// mounting the disk images (DDI) specified in `RootImage=`, `MountImage=`,
/// `ExtensionImage=`, respectively. If not specified the following policy
/// string is the default for `RootImagePolicy=` and `MountImagePolicy`:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         home=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         srv=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         tmp=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         var=encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// The default policy for `ExtensionImagePolicy=` is:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// See [MountImagePolicy=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootImagePolicy=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 254.
mountImagePolicy: String?

/// Takes an image policy string as per systemd.image-policy(7) to use when
/// mounting the disk images (DDI) specified in `RootImage=`, `MountImage=`,
/// `ExtensionImage=`, respectively. If not specified the following policy
/// string is the default for `RootImagePolicy=` and `MountImagePolicy`:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         home=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         srv=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         tmp=encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         var=encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// The default policy for `ExtensionImagePolicy=` is:
///
/// ```
/// root=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent: \
///         usr=verity+signed+encrypted+unprotected+absent
/// ```
///
/// See [ExtensionImagePolicy=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootImagePolicy=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 254.
extensionImagePolicy: String?

/// Takes a path to a systemd.mstack(7) directory encapsulating a mount stack
/// consisting of layers and bind mounts. Similar to `RootDirectory=` and
/// `RootImage=` this runs the service off a distinct root file system, in this
/// case set up via `overlayfs`.
///
/// Since `.mstack/` directories may reference disk images (DDIs) similar
/// device policy extensions and dependencies are in effect when `RootMStack=`
/// is used as are if `RootImage=` is used.
///
/// In place of the image path a `.v/` versioned directory may be specified,
/// see systemd.v(7) for details.
///
/// When enabled for services running in per-user instances of the service
/// manager this option implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` (requires
/// unprivileged user namespaces support to be enabled in the kernel via the
/// `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=` sysctl) and also relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8).
///
/// See [RootMStack=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#RootMStack=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 260.
rootMStack: String?

/// Takes a boolean argument. If on, a private mount namespace for the unit's
/// processes is created and the API file systems `/proc/`, `/sys/`, `/dev/`
/// and `/run/` (as an empty `tmpfs`) are mounted inside of it, unless they are
/// already mounted. Note that this option has no effect unless used in
/// conjunction with `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` as these four mounts are
/// generally mounted in the host anyway, and unless the root directory is
/// changed, the private mount namespace will be a 1:1 copy of the host's, and
/// include these four mounts. Note that the `/dev/` file system of the host is
/// bind mounted if this option is used without `PrivateDevices=`. To run the
/// service with a private, minimal version of `/dev/`, combine this option
/// with `PrivateDevices=`.
///
/// In order to allow propagating mounts at runtime in a safe manner,
/// `/run/systemd/propagate/` on the host will be used to set up new mounts,
/// and `/run/host/incoming/` in the private namespace will be used as an
/// intermediate step to store them before being moved to the final mount
/// point.
///
/// See [MountAPIVFS=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#MountAPIVFS=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 233.
mountAPIVFS: Boolean?

/// Takes a boolean argument. If true, sockets from systemd-journald.socket(8)
/// will be bind mounted into the mount namespace. This is particularly useful
/// when a different instance of `/run/` is employed, to make sure processes
/// running in the namespace can still make use of sd-journal(3).
///
/// This option is implied when `LogNamespace=` is used, when
/// `MountAPIVFS=yes`, or when `PrivateDevices=yes` is used in conjunction with
/// either `RootDirectory=` or `RootImage=`.
///
/// See [BindLogSockets=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#BindLogSockets=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 257.
bindLogSockets: Boolean?

/// Takes one of `noaccess`, `invisible`, `ptraceable` or `default` (which it
/// defaults to). When set, this controls the `hidepid=` mount option of the
/// `procfs` instance for the unit that controls which directories with process
/// metainformation (`/proc/PID`) are visible and accessible: when set to
/// `noaccess` the ability to access most of other users' process metadata in
/// `/proc/` is taken away for processes of the service. When set to
/// `invisible` processes owned by other users are hidden from `/proc/`. If
/// `ptraceable` all processes that cannot be `ptrace()`'ed by a process are
/// hidden to it. If `default` no restrictions on `/proc/` access or visibility
/// are made. For further details see [The /proc
/// Filesystem](https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html#mount-options).
/// It is generally recommended to run most system services with this option
/// set to `invisible`. This option is implemented via file system namespacing,
/// and thus cannot be used with services that shall be able to install mount
/// points in the host file system hierarchy. Note that the root user is
/// unaffected by this option, so to be effective it has to be used together
/// with `User=` or `DynamicUser=yes`, and also without the `CAP_SYS_PTRACE`
/// capability, which also allows a process to bypass this feature. It cannot
/// be used for services that need to access metainformation about other users'
/// processes. This option implies `MountAPIVFS=`.
///
/// If the kernel does not support per-mount point `hidepid=` mount options
/// this setting remains without effect, and the unit's processes will be able
/// to access and see other process as if the option was not used.
///
/// This option is only available for system services and is not supported for
/// services running in per-user instances of the service manager.
///
/// See [ProtectProc=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#ProtectProc=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 247.
protectProc: ProtectProc?

/// Takes one of `all` (the default) and `pid`. If `pid`, all files and
/// directories not directly associated with process management and
/// introspection are made invisible in the `/proc/` file system configured for
/// the unit's processes. This controls the `subset=` mount option of the
/// `procfs` instance for the unit. For further details see [The /proc
/// Filesystem](https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html#mount-options).
/// Note that Linux exposes various kernel APIs via `/proc/`, which are made
/// unavailable with this setting. Since these APIs are used frequently this
/// option is useful only in a few, specific cases, and is not suitable for
/// most non-trivial programs.
///
/// Much like `ProtectProc=` above, this is implemented via file system mount
/// namespacing, and hence the same restrictions apply: it is only available to
/// system services, it disables mount propagation to the host mount table, and
/// it implies `MountAPIVFS=`. Also, like `ProtectProc=` this setting is
/// gracefully disabled if the used kernel does not support the `subset=` mount
/// option of `procfs`.
///
/// See [ProcSubset=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#ProcSubset=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 247.
procSubset: ProcSubset?

/// Configures unit-specific bind mounts, which make a file or directory
/// available at an additional place in the unit's view of the file system,
/// without being visible in the host's mount table. The bind mounts are
/// writable, unless the source file system mount is already marked read-only;
/// see `BindReadOnlyPaths=` for read-only bind mounts.
///
/// Each definition consists of a colon-separated triple of source path,
/// destination path and option string, where the latter two are optional. If
/// only a source path is specified, the destination is taken to be the same.
/// The option string may be either `rbind` or `norbind`, for a recursive or
/// non-recursive bind mount. A definition may be prefixed with `-`, in which
/// case it is ignored when its source path does not exist or is not
/// accessible.
///
/// Using this option implies the effect of `PrivateMounts=`. It is
/// particularly useful when `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` is used: in this
/// case, the source path refers to a path on the host file system, while the
/// destination path refers to a path below the root directory of the unit.
///
/// The destination directory must exist or systemd must be able to create it.
/// Thus, it is not possible to use this option for mount points nested
/// underneath paths specified in `InaccessiblePaths=`, or under `/home/` and
/// other protected directories if `ProtectHome=yes` is specified.
/// `TemporaryFileSystem=` with `:ro` or `ProtectHome=tmpfs` should be used
/// instead.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `BindPaths=` line.
///
/// See [BindPaths=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#BindPaths=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 233.
bindPaths: Listing<String>?

/// Configures unit-specific read-only bind mounts, which make a file or
/// directory available at an additional place in the unit's view of the file
/// system, without being visible in the host's mount table. See `BindPaths=`
/// for writable bind mounts.
///
/// Each definition consists of a colon-separated triple of source path,
/// destination path and option string, where the latter two are optional. If
/// only a source path is specified, the destination is taken to be the same.
/// The option string may be either `rbind` or `norbind`, for a recursive or
/// non-recursive bind mount. A definition may be prefixed with `-`, in which
/// case it is ignored when its source path does not exist or is not
/// accessible.
///
/// Using this option implies the effect of `PrivateMounts=`. It is
/// particularly useful when `RootDirectory=`/`RootImage=` is used: in this
/// case, the source path refers to a path on the host file system, while the
/// destination path refers to a path below the root directory of the unit.
///
/// The destination directory must exist or systemd must be able to create it.
/// Thus, it is not possible to use this option for mount points nested
/// underneath paths specified in `InaccessiblePaths=`, or under `/home/` and
/// other protected directories if `ProtectHome=yes` is specified.
/// `TemporaryFileSystem=` with `:ro` or `ProtectHome=tmpfs` should be used
/// instead.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `BindReadOnlyPaths=`
/// line.
///
/// See [BindReadOnlyPaths=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#BindPaths=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 233.
bindReadOnlyPaths: Listing<String>?

/// This setting is similar to `RootImage=` in that it mounts a file system
/// hierarchy from a block device node or loopback file, but the destination
/// directory can be specified as well as mount options. The mounts are
/// specific to the unit, and are not visible in the host's mount table.
///
/// Each definition consists of a colon-separated tuple of source path and
/// destination path, optionally followed by another colon and mount options.
/// The mount options are either a comma-separated list of options, which apply
/// to the root partition of the image, or a series of colon-separated tuples
/// of partition name and mount options, as for `RootImageOptions=`. The source
/// is a path to a block device node or regular file, which follows the same
/// rules as for `RootImage=`. A `:` in the source or destination needs to be
/// escaped as `\:`. A definition may be prefixed with `-`, in which case it is
/// ignored when its source path does not exist.
///
/// The destination directory must exist or systemd must be able to create it.
/// Thus, it is not possible to use this option for mount points nested
/// underneath paths specified in `InaccessiblePaths=`, or under `/home/` and
/// other protected directories if `ProtectHome=yes` is specified.
///
/// When enabled for services of the per-user service manager, this option
/// implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` and relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8), and mount options are disabled by default.
/// When `DevicePolicy=` restricts device access, access to the loop devices is
/// added to `DeviceAllow=`.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `MountImages=` line.
///
/// See [MountImages=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#MountImages=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 247.
mountImages: Listing<String>?

/// This setting is similar to `MountImages=` in that it mounts a file system
/// hierarchy from a block device node or loopback file, but instead of
/// providing a destination path, an overlay is set up: a read-only OverlayFS
/// on top of `/usr/` and `/opt/` for sysext images, and of `/etc/` for confext
/// images. Images listed first to last result in overlayfs layers bottom to
/// top. The mounts are specific to the unit, and are not visible in the host's
/// mount table.
///
/// Each definition consists of a source path, optionally followed by a colon
/// and mount options: either a comma-separated list of options, which apply to
/// the root partition of the image, or a series of colon-separated tuples of
/// partition name and mount options, as for `RootImageOptions=`. The source
/// follows the same rules as for `RootImage=`, and may be a `.v/` versioned
/// directory, see systemd.v(7). A `:` in it needs to be escaped as `\:`. A
/// definition may be prefixed with `-`, in which case it is ignored when its
/// source path does not exist.
///
/// Each sysext must carry a
/// `/usr/lib/extension-release.d/extension-release.IMAGE` file, and each
/// confext a `/etc/extension-release.d/extension-release.IMAGE` file, with
/// metadata matching `RootImage=`/`RootDirectory=` or the host, see
/// os-release(5). If `RefreshOnReload=extensions` is enabled (the default),
/// versioned confexts are refreshed on service reload.
///
/// When enabled for services of the per-user service manager, this option
/// implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` and relies on
/// systemd-mountfsd.service(8), and mount options are disabled by default.
/// When `DevicePolicy=` restricts device access, access to the loop devices is
/// added to `DeviceAllow=`.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `ExtensionImages=`
/// line.
///
/// See [ExtensionImages=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#ExtensionImages=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 248.
extensionImages: Listing<String>?

/// This setting is similar to `BindReadOnlyPaths=` in that it mounts a file
/// system hierarchy from a directory, but instead of providing a destination
/// path, an overlay is set up: a read-only OverlayFS on top of `/usr/` and
/// `/opt/` for sysext directories, and of `/etc/` for confext directories.
/// Directories listed first to last result in overlayfs layers bottom to top.
/// The mounts are specific to the unit, and are not visible in the host's
/// mount table.
///
/// A directory may be prefixed with `-`, in which case it is ignored when it
/// does not exist. In place of the directory path a `.v/` versioned directory
/// may be specified, see systemd.v(7).
///
/// Each sysext must carry a
/// `/usr/lib/extension-release.d/extension-release.IMAGE` file, and each
/// confext a `/etc/extension-release.d/extension-release.IMAGE` file, with
/// metadata matching `RootImage=`/`RootDirectory=` or the host, see
/// os-release(5). If `RefreshOnReload=extensions` is enabled (the default),
/// versioned confexts are refreshed on service reload.
///
/// This option is only available for system services, or for services of the
/// per-user service manager, where it implicitly enables `PrivateUsers=` and
/// requires overlayfs support in unprivileged user namespaces (kernel v5.11).
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate
/// `ExtensionDirectories=` line.
///
/// See [ExtensionDirectories=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#ExtensionDirectories=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 251.
extensionDirectories: Listing<String>?

/// Set the UNIX user or group that the processes are executed as,
/// respectively. Takes a single user or group name, or a numeric ID as
/// argument. For system services (services run by the system service manager,
/// i.e. managed by PID 1) and for user services of the root user (services
/// managed by root's instance of **systemd --user**), the default is `root`,
/// but `User=` may be used to specify a different user. For user services of
/// any other user, switching user identity is not permitted, hence the only
/// valid setting is the same user the user's service manager is running as. If
/// no group is set, the default group of the user is used. This setting does
/// not affect commands whose command line is prefixed with `+`.
///
/// Note that this enforces only weak restrictions on the user/group name
/// syntax, but will generate warnings in many cases where user/group names do
/// not adhere to the following rules: the specified name should consist only
/// of the characters a-z, A-Z, 0-9, `_` and `-`, except for the first
/// character which must be one of a-z, A-Z and `_` (i.e. digits and `-` are
/// not permitted as first character). The user/group name must have at least
/// one character, and at most 31. These restrictions are made in order to
/// avoid ambiguities and to ensure user/group names and unit files remain
/// portable among Linux systems. For further details on the names accepted and
/// the names warned about see [User/Group Name
/// Syntax](https://systemd.io/USER_NAMES).
///
/// When used in conjunction with `DynamicUser=` the user/group name specified
/// is dynamically allocated at the time the service is started, and released
/// at the time the service is stopped — unless it is already allocated
/// statically (see below). If `DynamicUser=` is not used the specified user
/// and group must have been created statically in the user database no later
/// than the moment the service is started, for example using the sysusers.d(5)
/// facility, which is applied at boot or package install time. If the user
/// does not exist by then program invocation will fail.
///
/// If the `User=` setting is used the supplementary group list is initialized
/// from the specified user's default group list, as defined in the system's
/// user and group database. Additional groups may be configured through the
/// `SupplementaryGroups=` setting (see below).
///
/// See [User=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#User=) in systemd.exec(5).
user: String?

/// Set the UNIX user or group that the processes are executed as,
/// respectively. Takes a single user or group name, or a numeric ID as
/// argument. For system services (services run by the system service manager,
/// i.e. managed by PID 1) and for user services of the root user (services
/// managed by root's instance of **systemd --user**), the default is `root`,
/// but `User=` may be used to specify a different user. For user services of
/// any other user, switching user identity is not permitted, hence the only
/// valid setting is the same user the user's service manager is running as. If
/// no group is set, the default group of the user is used. This setting does
/// not affect commands whose command line is prefixed with `+`.
///
/// Note that this enforces only weak restrictions on the user/group name
/// syntax, but will generate warnings in many cases where user/group names do
/// not adhere to the following rules: the specified name should consist only
/// of the characters a-z, A-Z, 0-9, `_` and `-`, except for the first
/// character which must be one of a-z, A-Z and `_` (i.e. digits and `-` are
/// not permitted as first character). The user/group name must have at least
/// one character, and at most 31. These restrictions are made in order to
/// avoid ambiguities and to ensure user/group names and unit files remain
/// portable among Linux systems. For further details on the names accepted and
/// the names warned about see [User/Group Name
/// Syntax](https://systemd.io/USER_NAMES).
///
/// When used in conjunction with `DynamicUser=` the user/group name specified
/// is dynamically allocated at the time the service is started, and released
/// at the time the service is stopped — unless it is already allocated
/// statically (see below). If `DynamicUser=` is not used the specified user
/// and group must have been created statically in the user database no later
/// than the moment the service is started, for example using the sysusers.d(5)
/// facility, which is applied at boot or package install time. If the user
/// does not exist by then program invocation will fail.
///
/// If the `User=` setting is used the supplementary group list is initialized
/// from the specified user's default group list, as defined in the system's
/// user and group database. Additional groups may be configured through the
/// `SupplementaryGroups=` setting (see below).
///
/// See [Group=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#User=) in systemd.exec(5).
group: String?

/// Takes a boolean parameter. If set, a UNIX user and group pair is allocated
/// dynamically when the unit is started, and released as soon as it is
/// stopped. The user and group are not added to `/etc/passwd` or `/etc/group`,
/// but are managed transiently during runtime, and integrated into the user
/// and group databases by nss-systemd(8). Their name may be configured via
/// `User=` and `Group=`, and is otherwise derived from the unit name. If a
/// statically allocated user or group of that name already exists, it is used
/// instead.
///
/// Dynamic users/groups are allocated from the UID/GID range 61184…65519, and
/// UID/GIDs are recycled after a unit is terminated. The unit's processes must
/// not leave files or directories owned by them around, as a different unit
/// might get the same UID/GID assigned later on, and thus gain access to them.
/// To ensure this, this option implies:
///
/// - `RemoveIPC=`, `NoNewPrivileges=` and `RestrictSUIDSGID=`, which cannot be
///   turned off;
/// - `PrivateTmp=disconnected`, unless `PrivateTmp=` is set to true;
/// - `ProtectSystem=strict` and `ProtectHome=read-only`.
///
/// Use `RuntimeDirectory=`, `StateDirectory=`, `CacheDirectory=` and
/// `LogsDirectory=` to assign writable directories to the service in a way
/// that is protected from vulnerabilities due to UID reuse. Do not give the
/// unit's processes access to other writable directories: specifically, do not
/// use `BindPaths=`, and be careful with `ReadWritePaths=` and with passing
/// directory file descriptors over `AF_UNIX` sockets.
///
/// This option is currently incompatible with D-Bus policies, thus a service
/// using it may not allocate a D-Bus service name. Defaults to off.
///
/// See [DynamicUser=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#DynamicUser=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
///
/// Added in version 232.
dynamicUser: Boolean?

/// Sets the supplementary Unix groups the processes are executed as. This
/// takes a space-separated list of group names or IDs. This option may be
/// specified more than once, in which case all listed groups are set as
/// supplementary groups. When the empty string is assigned, the list of
/// supplementary groups is reset, and all assignments prior to this one will
/// have no effect. In any way, this option does not override, but extends the
/// list of supplementary groups configured in the system group database for
/// the user. This does not affect commands prefixed with `+`.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate
/// `SupplementaryGroups=` line.
///
/// See [SupplementaryGroups=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#SupplementaryGroups=) in systemd.exec(5).
supplementaryGroups: Listing<String>?

/// Takes a boolean parameter that controls whether to set the `$HOME`,
/// `$LOGNAME`, and `$SHELL` environment variables. If not set, this defaults
/// to true if `User=`, `DynamicUser=` or `PAMName=` are set, false otherwise.
/// If set to true, the variables will always be set for system services, i.e.
/// even when the default user `root` is used. If set to false, the mentioned
/// variables are not set by the service manager, no matter whether `User=`,
/// `DynamicUser=`, or `PAMName=` are used or not. This option normally has no
/// effect on services of the per-user service manager, since in that case
/// these variables are typically inherited from user manager's own environment
/// anyway.
///
/// See [SetLoginEnvironment=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#SetLoginEnvironment=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 255.
setLoginEnvironment: Boolean?

/// Sets the PAM service name to set up a session as. If set, the executed
/// process will be registered as a PAM session under the specified service
/// name. This is only useful in conjunction with the `User=` setting, and is
/// otherwise ignored. If not set, no PAM session will be opened for the
/// executed processes. See pam(8) for details.
///
/// Note that for each unit making use of this option a PAM session handler
/// process will be maintained as part of the unit and stays around as long as
/// the unit is active, to ensure that appropriate actions can be taken when
/// the unit and hence the PAM session terminates. This process is named
/// `(sd-pam)` and is an immediate child process of the unit's main process.
///
/// Note that when this option is used for a unit it is very likely (depending
/// on PAM configuration) that the main unit process will be migrated to its
/// own session scope unit when it is activated. This process will hence be
/// associated with two units: the unit it was originally started from (and for
/// which `PAMName=` was configured), and the session scope unit. Any child
/// processes of that process will however be associated with the session scope
/// unit only. This has implications when used in combination with
/// `NotifyAccess=all`, as these child processes will not be able to affect
/// changes in the original unit through notification messages. These messages
/// will be considered belonging to the session scope unit and not the original
/// unit. It is hence not recommended to use `PAMName=` in combination with
/// `NotifyAccess=all`.
///
/// If a PAM module interactively requests input (a password or suchlike) it
/// will be attempted to be read from a service credential (as configured via
/// `SetCredential=`, `ImportCredential=` and related calls) under the name
/// `pam.authtok.pamservice`, where *pamservice* is replaced by the PAM service
/// name as configured with `PAMName=`. (Note that the credential remains
/// accessible for the runtime of the service!) If no matching credential is
/// set, the user is prompted for it interactively via the [Password
/// Agent](https://systemd.io/PASSWORD_AGENTS) logic.
///
/// See [PAMName=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#PAMName=) in systemd.exec(5).
@Unit.Key { name = "PAMName" }
pamName: String?

/// Controls which capabilities to include in the capability bounding set for
/// the executed process, see capabilities(7). Takes a list of capability
/// names, e.g. `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, `CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`, `CAP_SYS_PTRACE`. The
/// capabilities listed are included in the bounding set, and all others are
/// removed. If the list is prefixed with `~`, the effect is inverted: all but
/// the listed capabilities are included. This also affects the respective
/// capabilities in the effective, permitted and inheritable capability sets.
///
/// If this option is not used, the capability bounding set is not modified on
/// process execution, hence no limits on the capabilities of the process are
/// enforced. It does not affect commands prefixed with `+`.
///
/// The elements of the listing are merged by `OR`, or by `AND` if prefixed
/// with `~`: `CAP_A CAP_B` followed by `CAP_B CAP_C` sets `CAP_A`, `CAP_B` and
/// `CAP_C`, while `CAP_A CAP_B` followed by `~CAP_B CAP_C` only sets `CAP_A`.
/// An empty element resets the bounding set to the empty capability set, and
/// `~` alone resets it to the full set of available capabilities.
///
/// Use the **capability** command of systemd-analyze(1) to retrieve a list of
/// capabilities defined on the local system.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate
/// `CapabilityBoundingSet=` line.
///
/// See [CapabilityBoundingSet=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#CapabilityBoundingSet=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
capabilityBoundingSet: Listing<String>?

/// Controls which capabilities to include in the ambient capability set for
/// the executed process. Takes a whitespace-separated list of capability
/// names, e.g. `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, `CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`, `CAP_SYS_PTRACE`. This
/// option may appear more than once, in which case the ambient capability sets
/// are merged (see the above examples in `CapabilityBoundingSet=`). If the
/// list of capabilities is prefixed with `~`, all but the listed capabilities
/// will be included, the effect of the assignment inverted. If the empty
/// string is assigned to this option, the ambient capability set is reset to
/// the empty capability set, and all prior settings have no effect. If set to
/// `~` (without any further argument), the ambient capability set is reset to
/// the full set of available capabilities, also undoing any previous settings.
/// Note that adding capabilities to the ambient capability set adds them to
/// the process's inherited capability set.
///
/// Ambient capability sets are useful if you want to execute a process as a
/// non-privileged user but still want to give it some capabilities. Note that,
/// in this case, option `keep-caps` is automatically added to `SecureBits=` to
/// retain the capabilities over the user change. `AmbientCapabilities=` does
/// not affect commands prefixed with `+`.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate
/// `AmbientCapabilities=` line.
///
/// See [AmbientCapabilities=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#AmbientCapabilities=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 229.
ambientCapabilities: Listing<String>?

/// Takes a boolean argument. If true, ensures that the service process and all
/// its children can never gain new privileges through `execve()` (e.g. via
/// setuid or setgid bits, or filesystem capabilities). This is the simplest
/// and most effective way to ensure that a process and its children can never
/// elevate privileges again. Defaults to false. In case the service will be
/// run in a new mount namespace anyway and SELinux is disabled, all file
/// systems are mounted with `MS_NOSUID` flag. Also see [No New Privileges
/// Flag](https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/no_new_privs.html).
///
/// Note that this setting only has an effect on the unit's processes
/// themselves (or any processes directly or indirectly forked off them). It
/// has no effect on processes potentially invoked on request of them through
/// tools such as at(1), crontab(1), systemd-run(1), or arbitrary IPC services.
///
/// See [NoNewPrivileges=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#NoNewPrivileges=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 187.
noNewPrivileges: Boolean?

/// Controls the secure bits set for the executed process. Takes a
/// space-separated combination of options from the following list:
/// `keep-caps`, `keep-caps-locked`, `no-setuid-fixup`,
/// `no-setuid-fixup-locked`, `noroot`, `noroot-locked`,
/// `no-cap-ambient-raise`, `no-cap-ambient-raise-locked`,
/// `exec-restrict-file`, `exec-restrict-file-locked`, `exec-deny-interactive`,
/// and `exec-deny-interactive-locked`. This option may appear more than once,
/// in which case the secure bits are ORed. If the empty string is assigned to
/// this option, the bits are reset to 0. This does not affect commands
/// prefixed with `+`. See capabilities(7) and [Executability
/// check](https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/check_exec.html) for details.
///
/// Each element of the listing is rendered as a separate `SecureBits=` line.
///
/// See [SecureBits=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#SecureBits=) in systemd.exec(5).
secureBits: Listing<String>?

/// Set the SELinux security context of the executed process. If set, this will
/// override the automated domain transition. However, the policy still needs
/// to authorize the transition. This directive is ignored if SELinux is
/// disabled. If prefixed by `-`, failing to set the SELinux security context
/// will be ignored, but it is still possible that the subsequent `execve()`
/// may fail if the policy does not allow the transition for the non-overridden
/// context. This does not affect commands prefixed with `+`. See setexeccon(3)
/// for details.
///
/// See [SELinuxContext=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#SELinuxContext=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 209.
@Unit.Key { name = "SELinuxContext" }
selinuxContext: String?

/// Takes a profile name as argument. The process executed by the unit will
/// switch to this profile when started. Profiles must already be loaded in the
/// kernel, or the unit will fail. If prefixed by `-`, all errors will be
/// ignored. This setting has no effect if AppArmor is not enabled. This
/// setting does not affect commands prefixed with `+`.
///
/// This option is only available for system services and is not supported for
/// services running in per-user instances of the service manager.
///
/// See [AppArmorProfile=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#AppArmorProfile=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 210.
appArmorProfile: String?

/// Takes a `SMACK64` security label as argument. The process executed by the
/// unit will be started under this label and SMACK will decide whether the
/// process is allowed to run or not, based on it. The process will continue to
/// run under the label specified here unless the executable has its own
/// `SMACK64EXEC` label, in which case the process will transition to run under
/// that label. When not specified, the label that systemd is running under is
/// used. This directive is ignored if SMACK is disabled.
///
/// The value may be prefixed by `-`, in which case all errors will be ignored.
/// An empty value may be specified to unset previous assignments. This does
/// not affect commands prefixed with `+`.
///
/// This option is only available for system services and is not supported for
/// services running in per-user instances of the service manager.
///
/// See [SmackProcessLabel=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#SmackProcessLabel=) in systemd.exec(5).
///
/// Added in version 218.
smackProcessLabel: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the CPU time an executed process may
/// consume. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -t** shell command. See
/// setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitCPU=30s:1min`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The usual time units ms, s,
/// min, h and so on may be used (see systemd.time(7) for details). If no time
/// unit is specified, the default unit of seconds is implied. Note that the
/// effective granularity of the limit might influence its enforcement: time
/// limits specified for `LimitCPU=` will be rounded up implicitly to multiples
/// of 1s.
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitCPU=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if not
/// configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitCPU=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitCPU: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the size of the files an executed process
/// may create. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -f** shell command. See
/// setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitFSIZE=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitFSIZE=16G`).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitFSIZE=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if
/// not configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitFSIZE=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitFSIZE: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the data segment size of an executed
/// process. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -d** shell command. See
/// setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitDATA=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitDATA=16G`).
///
/// Do not use. This limits the allowed address range, not memory use! Defaults
/// to unlimited and should not be lowered. To limit memory use, see
/// `MemoryMax=` in systemd.resource-control(5).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitDATA=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if not
/// configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitDATA=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitDATA: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the stack size of an executed process.
/// This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -s** shell command. See setrlimit(2)
/// for details on the process resource limit concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitSTACK=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitSTACK=16G`).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitSTACK=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if
/// not configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitSTACK=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitSTACK: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the size of the core file an executed
/// process may dump. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -c** shell
/// command. See setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit
/// concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitCORE=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitCORE=16G`).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitCORE=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if not
/// configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitCORE=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitCORE: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the resident set size of an executed
/// process. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -m** shell command. See
/// setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitRSS=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitRSS=16G`).
///
/// Do not use. This limit is not implemented on Linux, and setting it has no
/// effect. `MemoryMax=` (see systemd.resource-control(5)) is a more powerful
/// (and working) replacement.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitRSS=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if not
/// configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitRSS=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitRSS: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the number of file descriptors an executed
/// process may open. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -n** shell
/// command. See setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit
/// concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitNOFILE=1024:4096`).
/// Use the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is a plain
/// number.
///
/// Do not use. Be careful when raising the soft limit above 1024, since
/// select(2) cannot function with file descriptors above 1023 on Linux. The
/// hard limit nowadays defaults to 524288, and applications that do not use
/// select(2) should raise their soft limit to it on their own. There should
/// not be any need to lower the hard limit, as file descriptors are accounted
/// like any other form of memory: use `MemoryMax=` to control overall service
/// memory use.
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitNOFILE=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if
/// not configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitNOFILE=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitNOFILE: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the address space (virtual memory) size of
/// an executed process. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -v** shell
/// command. See setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit
/// concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitAS=4G:16G`). Use
/// the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitAS=16G`).
///
/// Do not use. This limits the allowed address range, not memory use! Defaults
/// to unlimited and should not be lowered. To limit memory use, see
/// `MemoryMax=` in systemd.resource-control(5).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitAS=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if not
/// configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitAS=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitAS: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the number of processes of the user the
/// executed processes run as. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -u**
/// shell command. See setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit
/// concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitNPROC=1024:4096`).
/// Use the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is a plain
/// number.
///
/// Note that `LimitNPROC=` will limit the number of processes from one (real)
/// UID and not the number of processes started (forked) by the service.
/// Therefore the limit is cumulative for all processes running under the same
/// UID. Please also note that the `LimitNPROC=` will not be enforced if the
/// service is running as root (and not dropping privileges). Due to these
/// limitations, `TasksMax=` (see systemd.resource-control(5)) is typically a
/// better choice than `LimitNPROC=`.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
///
/// If not configured explicitly for a unit, the limit defaults to the value
/// configured with `DefaultLimitNPROC=` in systemd-system.conf(5), and – if
/// not configured there – the kernel or per-user default, as defined by the OS
/// (the latter only for user services).
///
/// In a user service (i.e. a service run by the per-user instance of the
/// service manager) this option cannot raise the limit above the one set for
/// the user manager itself: it is only useful to lower the limit, or to raise
/// the soft limit up to the hard limit.
///
/// See [LimitNPROC=](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html#LimitCPU=) in systemd.exec(5) for the full details.
limitNPROC: String?

/// Sets the soft and hard limits on the amount of memory an executed process
/// may lock into RAM. This is the equivalent of the **ulimit -l** shell
/// command. See setrlimit(2) for details on the process resource limit
/// concept.
///
/// The limit may be specified in two formats: either as single value to set
/// the soft and hard limit to the same value, or as colon-separated pair
/// `soft:hard` to set both limits individually (e.g. `LimitMEMLOCK=4G:16G`).
/// Use the string `infinity` to configure no limit. The value is measured in
/// bytes, and the multiplicative suffixes K, M, G, T, P and E (to the base
/// 1024) may be used (e.g. `LimitMEMLOCK=16G`).
///
/// Note that this limit is per-process, and processes may fork in order to
/// escape it. Often it is advisable to prefer the resource controls listed in
/// systemd.resource-control(5), as they apply to services as a whole and may
/// be altered dynamically at runtime.
///
/// This setting, including its default, does not affect commands prefixed with
/// `+`, which inherit the resource limits of the service manager itself.
